  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using DNA metabarcoding, this study characterized bacterial and fungal soil communities across five urban typologies, including open tree pits with Tilia trees, soils beneath sidewalks, and soils under roadways, both with and without nearby trees, across five Dutch cities. While bacterial alpha diversity remained consistent across all typologies, fungal richness was 91% higher near street trees and 304% higher in open tree pits compared to areas without trees. The presence of trees also correlated with an overrepresentation of putative fungal traits and a higher abundance of bacteria linked to nitrogen cycling.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that street trees and their immediate pits act as critical hotspots for microbial biodiversity and functional redundancy, showing that even highly confined urban greenspaces can mitigate the ecological degradation caused by extensive soil paving."
    }
  },
